 ## Overview
-The Efficient O(N) Distortion Loss is a regularization term for differentiable volume rendering that prevents "floaters" (small, disconnected regions of volumetrically dense space) and "background collapse" (distant surfaces modeled as semi-transparent clouds close to the camera) by minimizing the integral of distances between all pairs of points along the 1D step function parameterized by ray distances and weights [S1]. It is set apart from its siblings by its specific focus on geometric compactness along rays rather than spatial smoothness or photometric fidelity, and it distinguishes itself from the naive $O(N^2)$ formulation by achieving linear time complexity $O(N)$, enabling its use with high sample counts (>256 points per ray) without excessive GPU memory consumption [S2, unofficial].
+The Efficient O(N) Distortion Loss is a regularization term for differentiable volume rendering that prevents "floaters" (small, disconnected regions of volumetrically dense space) and "background collapse" (distant surfaces modeled as semi-transparent clouds close to the camera) [S1]. It minimizes the integral of distances between all pairs of points along the 1D step function parameterized by ray distances and weights [S1]. It sets itself apart from naive implementations by reducing computational complexity from $O(N^2)$ to $O(N)$, enabling the handling of point-based queries with large numbers of samples (e.g., >256) without excessive GPU memory consumption [S2, unofficial]. Unlike NeRF's noise injection regularizer, it more effectively eliminates artifacts without reducing reconstruction quality [S1].
 
