-**Edge Cases and Pitfalls**
-*   **Normalization:** Using unnormalized ray distances $t$ instead of normalized $s$ significantly upweights distant intervals, causing nearby intervals to be effectively ignored [S1].
-*   **Memory:** The naive $O(N^2)$ implementation consumes excessive GPU memory (>3GB for a batch of 4,096 rays with >256 samples per ray) [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Degenerate Input:** If $w_i = 0$ for all $i$, the loss is 0. The gradient is also 0. This is a valid minimum but may not be the desired solution if the ray passes through geometry [S1].
